#{[17592186416855 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416855 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416855 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416855 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following digraphs represent a reflexive relation defined on the set $A=\\{-1,0,1\\}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416855 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2cbb50f3-f95b-572e-a3ba-cdd7e80100c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416855 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nPairs of the form $(x,x)$ correspond to the loops that start and finish at the same node in the directed graphs.\n\nSince $R$ must be reflexive, we require that all nodes (vertices) have a loop. Among the given options, only the following digraph has this property:\n\n![](images/67/67e71a9121f014683271e905db7278ee6c11eeff31e707e45d70550042e44c2d.png)\n\n \n\nNote that all nodes contain a loop." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416856 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416856 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416856 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416856 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following digraphs represent a reflexive relation defined on the set $A=\\{\\pi ,\\omega ,ϕ\\}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416856 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c874a7f-ef16-5ee1-ac09-75fb14ed8d90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416856 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nPairs of the form $(x,x)$ correspond to the loops that start and finish at the same node in the directed graphs.\n\nSince $R$ must be reflexive, we require that all nodes (vertices) have a loop. Among the given options, only the following digraph has this property:\n\n![](images/9d/9db95cf46a9112d2f92e7ab9fe336b28d2da9c26971ba14360bdac772b6918e9.png)\n\n \n\nNote that all nodes contain a loop." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416857 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416857 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416857 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416857 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following relations are reflexive? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x≡2y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7),$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9),$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $y≡x+2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ defined on $Z$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416857 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "653fba49-a6b9-5e57-b514-cf99f2424cd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416857 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is neither reflexive nor irreflexive. Notice that \n\n- $(1,1)\\notin R$ since $1≢2(1)\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7),$ while\n\n- $(7,7)\\in R$ since $7≡2(7)\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).$\n\n- Relation $S$ is reflexive. Indeed, $(x,x)\\in S$ for all $x\\in Z.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≡x\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\n$$\n\nfor any $x\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is irreflexive. Indeed, $(x,x)\\notin T$ for all $x\\in Z.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≢x+2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)\n$$\n\nfor any $x\\in Z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416858 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416858 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416858 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following relations are reflexive? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x+y)∣10,$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $4∣({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $x∣(y+1),$ defined on $Z$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416858 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7095468b-6aec-5ce6-83d1-ea12403a6b5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416858 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is neither reflexive nor irreflexive. Notice that \n\n- $(2,2)\\notin R$ since $2+2=4$ and $4∤10,$ while\n\n- $(1,1)\\in R$ since $1+1=2$ and $2∣10.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is reflexive. Indeed, $(x,x)\\in S$ for all $x\\in Z.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n4∣({x}^{2}-{x}^{2})\n$$\n\nfor any $x\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is neither reflexive nor irreflexive. Notice that \n\n- $(2,2)\\notin T$ since $2∤(2+1),$ while\n\n- $(1,1)\\in T$ since $1∣(1+1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416859 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213251" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416859 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416859 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A=\\{5,8,10\\}.$ \n\n$$\nR=\\{(5,5),(5,8),(8,10),(10,5),(10,8)\\}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}, \n\nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416859 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c340422-8f3e-59cd-b595-ad2e94a755e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416859 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nIn our case, the relation is defined on $\\{5,8,10\\},$ and we have\n\n$$\n(5,5)\\in R,\\,(8,8)\\notin R,\\,(10,10)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\n$\\,$ Since $(8,8)\\notin R$ $\\text{and }(5,5)\\in R,$ the relation is $\\text{neither reflexive nor irreflexive}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416860 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416860 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213201" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416860 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416860 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $R$ be a relation defined on the set $R$ as follows: \n\n$\\,$ $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $y-{x}^{3}\\ne 0$ \n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}, \n\nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416860 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c3deb4f-bd86-5557-b5a7-4c0dc0f448ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416860 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(1,1)\\notin R$ since the pair does not satisfy the underlying condition:\n\n$$\n1-{1}^{3}=0\n$$\n\n- $(2,2)\\in R$ since the pair satisfies the underlying condition:\n\n$$\n2-{2}^{3}=-6\\ne 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\n$\\,$ Since $(1,1)\\notin R$ $\\text{and }(2,2)\\in R,$ the relation is $\\text{neither reflexive nor irreflexive}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416861 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416861 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416861 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416861 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213202" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416861 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416861 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $R$ be a relation defined on the set $Z$ as follows: \n\n$\\,$ $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $2∤(y-x)$ \n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}, \n\nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416861 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ccf91b35-7753-51f7-9fcf-6efffb54e68e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416861 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nThe relation $R$ is irreflexive. Indeed, since $2∣(x-x)$ for all $x\\in Z,$ we have $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in Z.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\n$\\,$ Since $(x,x)\\notin R$  $\\text{ for all }x\\in Z,$ the relation is $\\text{irreflexive}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416862 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416862 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416862 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416862 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416862 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416862 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416862 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416862 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213245" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416862 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416862 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A=\\{1,3,5,7\\}.$ \n\n$$\nR=\\{(1,3),(1,5),(3,5),(5,7)\\}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nWe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1,1){{field-1}}R,\\,(3,3){{field-2}}R,\\,(5,5){{field-3}}R,\\,(7,7){{field-4}}R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n{{field-5}}{{field-6}}, \n\nthe relation is \n{{field-7}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416862 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cfc59310-e932-539a-95d8-99d9f3c66e0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416862 #edb/ref 1074 "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nIn our case, the relation is defined on $\\{1,3,5,7\\},$ and we have\n\n$$\n(1,1)\\notin R,\\,(3,3)\\notin R,\\,(5,5)\\notin R,\\,(7,7)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\n$\\,$ Since $(x,x)\\notin R$ $\\text{for all }x\\in A,$ the relation is $\\text{irreflexive}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416863 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213228" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175